RAGE brief reports contain a statistical summary and comparison between measurements.
We want to test how this code behaves in various scenarios, and for that we need to generate data that we use as input.
However first lets examine the various random number generators that are available in OCaml and their quality.

{1 Random Number Generators }

We are going to use the generated test data to test various statistical functions, however any errors or biases in the generator may affect our test results.

{2 Testing the quality of OCaml's RNGs}

So first lets test the quality of the RNG using the {!module:TestU01} battery of tests (also part of `dieharder` test suite).
An OCaml module is available that wraps the testsuite, and executes tests sequentially, which can take a long time.
To speed up testing we can parallelize the test by running each test in a separate process, using the {!module:Parany} module.
The {!module:Testrng} wraps {!module:TestU01} and automatically parallelizes the tests, storing the output in a separate file for each test, and reporting any failures at the end.

OCaml has changed the implementation of its Random module between versions 4 and 5, we test both using the `stdlib-random` library which allows compiling the 2 versions independently of the compiler's version.

The results are in the table below:
{t
  | RNG                                                | Library        | Test duration | Test failures  |
  | :------------------------------------------------  | :------------: | ------------: | -------------: |
  | {!module:Random4}                                  | [stdlib-random]|          868s |            6 ✗ |
  | {!module:Random5}                                  | [stdlib-random]|         675.5s|            0 ✓ |
  | {!module:Owl_stats_prng} ([SFMT])                 | [owl]          |         637s  |            3 ✗ |
  | {!module:Cryptokit.Random.hardware_rng} ([rdrand]) | [cryptokit]    |               |            ?  |
  | {!module:Mirage_crypto_rng.Fortuna}                | [mirage-crypto-rng]|    856.2s+|            ?  |
}

The failures where p-values were outside of [[0.001, 0.9990]]:
{t
  | RNG       | Test battery  | Test number | Test name      | Parameters  | p-value |
  | :-------: | :-----------: | ----------: | :------------- | :---------- | ------: |
  | [Random4] | Crush         |        [32] | Gap            | [r = 27]    |   [eps] |
  | [Random4] | pseudoDIEHARD |         [7] | DNA            |             | [0.9992]|
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [35] | Gap            | [r = 25]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [61] | WeightDistrib  | [r = 28]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [64] | WeightDistrib  | [r = 26]    |    [eps] |
  | [Random4] | BigCrush      |        [98] | HammingIndep   |[L=300, r=26]|    [eps] |
  | [SFMT]    | pseudoDIEHARD |         [6] | OQSO           |             |  [0.990] |
  | [SFMT]    | BigCrush      |        [80] | LinearComp     | [r=0]       | [1-eps1] |
  | [SFMT]    | BigCrush      |        [81] | LinearComp     | [r=29]      | [1-eps1] |
 }

[SFMT] uses [sfmt_genrand_real1], which generates [double] with only 32-bit of precision, [sfmt_genrand_res53] would generate them with [53] bits of precision, but making that change doesn't fix the [LinearComp] failures, and switching to [dSFMT] instead of [SFMT] doesn't help. (The failures with a p-value of ~0.999 could be rerun, but those with a p-value of [eps] or [1-eps1] would almost always fail).

{!module:Random5} (the [Random] module in OCaml version 5) passed all tests, and is quicker than the OCaml 4 version too.

We'll use {!module:Random5} as our RNG.
